WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.470
Vsauce, Kevin here -- and this is you.

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:09.570
Your score is 14, because you’ve been unemployed
for 6 months and didn’t finish high school.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:15.019
Your next door neighbor is 23 because he was
arrested on a drug charge a few years back.

00:00:15.019 --> 00:00:19.400
Your cousin lives a block away -- he’s in
college, so he’s 2.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.130
Your mom’s 1, so is your sister.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:26.840
And then your best friend gets shot here in
a convenience store robbery -- all of a sudden

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.529
the police knock on your door to tell you
that a complex algorithm has placed you on

00:00:31.529 --> 00:00:36.210
a list of citizens at a high risk of being
involved in a shooting.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:42.230
But they also tell you they don’t know whether
you’ll be the shooter or the victim.

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:45.140
They claim they know everything about your
past.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:52.220
You’re under surveillance… because your
score, which was 14, is now 137.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:59.480
If that sounds too far-fetched to be true
-- ask Robert McDaniel.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:07.280
Their algorithm predicted his fate perfectly
-- or did they actually seal it?

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:15.610
In 2013, Chicago Police showed up uninvited
to tell Robert McDaniel that their algorithm

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:18.550
had determined he’d very likely be involved
in a shooting.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:22.329
I was sitting on the couch and they explained
to me, like, what was going on.

00:01:22.329 --> 00:01:24.000
This is what they’ve been looking for me
for.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.220
And they there telling me like I was most
likely to get shot or shoot somebody – of

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:28.250
my background.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.840
He was on their “Heat List.”

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:36.159
Each of 22 police precincts came up with 20
names of people most likely to be a party

00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:40.700
to crime -- and no one really knew the science
of it.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:46.290
My video about predictive policing detailed
the evolving effort to use data to guess where

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:52.210
crimes are most likely to occur, what types
of crimes they’ll be, and how to allocate

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.420
resources to reduce them.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:02:00.720
Chicago’s Strategic Subject List had hundreds
of thousands of names -- the “Heat List”

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.820
was the names at the very top.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.800
But for Robert McDaniel, it wasn’t about
patrolling a neighborhood that had some recent

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:08.800
break-ins.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.450
It was about watching one man on that list.

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:16.569
The problem wasn’t Robert’s criminal history.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:22.510
He’d been arrested before, but it was for
marijuana offenses and gambling with dice.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:25.090
The problem was his friends.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:30.950
Chicago received a $2 million grant from the
National Institute of Justice for a program

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:36.730
they called “Two Degrees of Association”:
an algorithm determined your level of crime

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:42.680
risk based not just on your own history, but
the actions of people you’d been arrested

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:43.930
with.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:51.290
You care about your friends, but the police
care about the hidden algebra of friendships.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:56.340
Robert had recently lost a friend to gun violence,
and he’d been arrested with that friend

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:59.370
in the past for marijuana use.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:04.769
Robert’s social proximity to a shooting
victim meant that his score on the Heat List

00:03:04.769 --> 00:03:13.680
was now 215 -- meaning the algorithm determined
that he was 215 times more likely than average

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.370
to be involved in a shooting.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:20.879
Some people on the Heat List had scores over
500.

00:03:20.879 --> 00:03:22.730
BOOBS.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:28.030
The director of the Medical Imaging Research
Center at the Illinois Institute of Technology

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:34.140
was involved in the algorithm, and he compared
predictive crime mapping to identifying anomalies

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.920
in mammograms.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.920
Look at the image and see what’s just…
not normal.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:44.610
In an interview with The Verge, designer Miles
Wernick said, "It’s not just shooting somebody,

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:46.230
or being shot.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:51.530
It has to do with the person’s relationships
to other violent people."

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.610
The algorithm is, again, a black box.

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:58.060
We don’t know any of the details of how
it works.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:03.780
But we do know that it’s based on social
network theory -- the police use data about

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:08.950
who you associate with to assess your risk
of engaging in crime.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.810
WHAT’S SOCIAL NETWORK THEORY?

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:13.690
Here’s a really specific example.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:18.049
Let’s say the new guy at work smokes alone
during his lunch break.

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:24.139
A former smoker might be influenced to start
smoking again and the two of them form a little

00:04:24.139 --> 00:04:26.150
smoke break club.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:32.110
A few other co-workers join in and now they’ve
created a smokers social network.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:38.759
THIS GUY is the catalyst for everyone else
changing their behavior and status -- including

00:04:38.759 --> 00:04:43.230
the ones who don’t smoke and are now suddenly
outsiders.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:50.470
Now two groups exist, smokers and non-smokers,
with varying individual behaviors, when before,

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:53.509
everyone just ate lunch together.

00:04:53.509 --> 00:04:56.419
Social influences spread like a virus.

00:04:56.419 --> 00:04:57.419
Hey!

00:04:57.419 --> 00:04:58.810
Real quick, I am a Lord.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.960
Look at this official framed documentation.

