00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:57.930
Okay.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:03.229
Andrew V. Papachristos and Christopher Wildeman
have posited that gun violence works similarly

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:08.320
to the transmission of blood-borne pathogens:
it’s an epidemic with patterns that can

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.760
be mapped just like cholera or HIV.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:18.910
They concluded that someone within two degrees
of a person involved in a shooting is 100

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:23.889
times more likely than average to be involved
in a future shooting.

00:06:23.889 --> 00:06:29.979
Using simple proximity as grounds for criminal
suspicion sounds a heckuva lot like guilt

00:06:29.979 --> 00:06:32.699
by association, RIGHT?

00:06:32.699 --> 00:06:35.300
WROo-... ehhh…

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:37.899
It’s complicated.

00:06:37.899 --> 00:06:43.270
The history of “joint enterprise” in British
Commonwealth law stretches back centuries

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:47.490
to address the rise in pistol dueling.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:53.180
Spectators were suddenly exposed to criminal
liability just as participants were, as their

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:58.860
role in encouraging the act was deemed a threat
to public safety.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.810
Is that really fair?

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:06.560
Well, for Robert McDaniel, the spectators
pulled the trigger.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:11.180
When police confronted him, they were accompanied
by a social worker who offered to help him

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:16.280
with employment or mental health services
to reduce his risk.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:18.280
That’s really good.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:21.650
Here’s what was really bad.

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:28.160
Neighbors who saw the encounter suspected
Robert of being a police informant, and knowing

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:34.040
he would be under police scrutiny despite
not having done anything wrong rightfully

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:36.919
confused and angered him.

00:07:36.919 --> 00:07:44.370
The Police trying to warn and aid him ended
up harming his social standing in the community.

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:48.949
There’s an old saying that “snitches get
stitches.”

00:07:48.949 --> 00:07:50.659
Robert got a bullet.

00:07:50.659 --> 00:07:56.729
A friend called Robert and asked him to explain
why he was on the Heat List, why he was talking

00:07:56.729 --> 00:08:02.819
to the police, and why journalists and documentarians
were interested in his life.

00:08:02.819 --> 00:08:06.900
When he came outside, a car pulled up and
shot him.

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:12.970
He didn’t report the shooting to police
because he said he’s not a snitch.

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:16.729
But by putting him on their Heat List – he
got heat.

00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:23.620
A 2016 paper said that Chicago’s Heat List
provided “no practical direction about what

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:30.550
to do with individuals [on it], little executive
or administrative attention paid to the pilot,

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:34.419
and little to no follow-up with district commanders.”

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:40.490
In 2020, Robert McDaniel was half a block
from his home when he was shot a second time

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:46.290
-- which he says is because his neighbors
still believe he’s working with police.

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:50.690
His reputation was destroyed for no benefit
to anyone.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:56.480
I just personally would like to wish Miss
Wesley the best of luck and hope they can

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:57.480
change something.

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.500
If I can do anything to help them, I’ll
help them.

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:04.920
But as far as them trying to make me a product
of their work?

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.200
I don’t too much appreciate that.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:12.390
High crime rates pushed police to transition
from being a reactive force responding to

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:17.399
crimes to a proactive force trying to prevent
them.

00:09:17.399 --> 00:09:22.880
They grabbed on to the data science of the
private sector because it worked in that sphere.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:27.430
But for law enforcement, the pipeline of responsibility
goes like this.

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:28.430
You wanna see the pipeline?

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:29.860
Here’s the pipeline.

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:36.400
A litany of government agencies collect data,
which is often flawed, incomplete or biased,

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:42.510
then mathematicians create algorithms using
that data, which programmers turn into usable

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:49.490
code, police departments try to apply the
output generally across cities, then individual

00:09:49.490 --> 00:09:56.670
officers implement results in specific neighborhoods,
and then courts interpret the whole chain

00:09:56.670 --> 00:10:01.790
of events -- so who’s actually responsible?

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:03.010
Everyone.

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:05.150
And no one.

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:11.480
Chicago ended the Heat List initiative 3 years
ago, but the London Metropolitan Police Force

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:17.790
continues to use and refine its own version
-- a database that combines personal information

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:24.300
with criminal associations to assign a green,
amber, or red designation to indicate a threat

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:25.720
of violence.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:28.339
They call it “The Matrix.”

00:10:28.339 --> 00:10:32.490
Which is totally not ominous at all.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:36.820
So what choices have YOU made that would put
you on the Heat List?

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:40.310
Would any of your friends make you more of
a suspect?

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:46.450
Have you STOPPED being friends with someone
because of their impact on your social status?

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:47.450
And…

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:52.660
what did that do to their life if you were
their only positive influence?

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:59.700
Do you have a moral responsibility to lower
the scores of people around you -- even if

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:01.930
you risk raising your own?

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:09.600
And if we can quantify these complex social
relationships, do we store the data?

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:10.779
How do we use it?

00:11:10.779 --> 00:11:12.900
WHO uses it, and for what?

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:14.070
To get a loan?

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:16.610
To have a social media account?

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:20.420
To hold a job, get married, order a pizza?

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:25.480
Which faceless authorities, each of whom is
completely divorced from the innerworkings

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:33.480
of the actual algorithm, are you comfortable
permanently etching a number into your identity?

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:38.000
… because once you’re on the list, there’s
no way off.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:43.150
And the number doesn’t care whether you
shoot the bullet or take it.

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:46.290
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:50.960
I say just best thing in life for you to do
is mind your own business.

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:54.329
Don’t bring problems to yourself that does
not need to be in your life.

